Notes

Files is the number of transcripts in ../output_stats.csv, which with the
camera selection in project_to_pose.sh should match the number of videos
processed, apart from the notes below. Trainslate and Justisigns are no longer
in output_stats.csv, so they are left out.

(1) InterGaze has 12 transcripts in output_stats.csv, each with a Camera_2 and
    a Camera_3 video, so 24 files and twice the transcripts' length (1:46:24).

(2) Stufen-CDs_Texte has 62 videos for 29 transcripts. The time assumes they
    are parts of the same recordings, so the total length stays the same.

(3) SMILE-Modellvideos currently processes all 1698 videos, including the
    downscaled copies (_160x90 ... _960x540). Its requested times assume about
    4 s per pose file and 8.5 s per segmentation, as for the other short-clip
    projects. Once the copies are excluded, 1 hour is enough for both.


How reliable the estimates are

- One short run is the only data. It doesn't separate the fixed cost per file
  (Python and the model starting up) from the cost per second of video, so pose
  times for projects with long videos (SMILE2, SMILE, SRT-2) are probably on
  the high side.
- Frame rate and resolution may differ. MediaPipe's cost depends on the number
  of frames, so projects recorded at a higher frame rate or resolution than the
  model videos will be slower.
- Memory is a guess. The pose data itself is small. Run seff <jobid> after the
  first jobs and lower --mem if peak use is well below; smaller requests
  usually get scheduled sooner.
